{:edb/db-before-t 341 :edb/db-after-t 342 :edb/tx-data [[13194139533654 50 #inst "2026-10-04T07:13:23.742Z" 13194139533654 true] [17592186129080 1020 #edb/ref 17592186244309 13194139533654 true] [17592186129080 1020 #edb/ref 17592186244316 13194139533654 true] [17592186129080 1020 #edb/ref 17592186244372 13194139533654 true] [17592186129080 1020 #edb/ref 17592186244393 13194139533654 true] [17592186129082 1020 #edb/ref 17592186244400 13194139533654 true] [17592186129082 1020 #edb/ref 17592186244407 13194139533654 true] [17592186129082 1020 #edb/ref 17592186244414 13194139533654 true] [17592186129089 1020 #edb/ref 17592186244302 13194139533654 true] [17592186129089 1020 #edb/ref 17592186244358 13194139533654 true] [17592186129089 1020 #edb/ref 17592186244365 13194139533654 true] [17592186129089 1020 #edb/ref 17592186244379 13194139533654 true] [17592186129089 1020 #edb/ref 17592186244386 13194139533654 true] [17592186129188 1020 #edb/ref 17592186244323 13194139533654 true] [17592186129188 1020 #edb/ref 17592186244330 13194139533654 true] [17592186129188 1020 #edb/ref 17592186244337 13194139533654 true] [17592186129188 1020 #edb/ref 17592186244344 13194139533654 true] [17592186129188 1020 #edb/ref 17592186244351 13194139533654 true] [17592186129620 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533654 true] [17592186129620 1145 "Based on the factored equation, we can make the following conclusions about the graph:\n\n- Since the multiplicity of the roots $x=0,\\pm 1$ is $1,$ which is odd, the graph must cross the $x$-axis at these points. Moreover, because $x=0,\\pm 1$ are simple roots, the graph must *not* be tangent to the $x$-axis at these points.\n\nOnly the graphs $\\text{II}$ and $\\text{IV}$ satisfy these conditions.\n\nTo choose between these two graphs, we need to consider the end behavior. Since the leading coefficient is positive, as $x$ approaches $\\infty ,$ $y$ approaches $\\infty .$ Since only graph $\\text{IV}$ displays this end behavior, the correct answer is $\\text{IV}.$" 13194139533654 true] [17592186129621 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533654 true] [17592186129621 1145 "Based on the factored equation, we can make the following conclusions about the graph:\n\n- Since the multiplicity of the root $x=2$ is $1,$ which is odd, the graph must cross the $x$-axis at this point. Moreover, because $x=2$ is a simple root, the graph must *not* be tangent to the $x$-axis at this point.\n\n- Since the multiplicity of the roots $x=0,1$ is $2,$ which is even, the graph just touches the $x$-axis and reverses direction at these points.\n\nOnly the graphs $\\text{I}$ and $\\text{IV}$ satisfy these conditions.\n\nTo choose between these two graphs, we need to consider the end behavior. Since the leading coefficient is negative, as $x$ approaches $\\infty ,$ $y$ approaches $-\\infty .$\n\nOnly graph $\\text{IV}$ displays this end behavior. Therefore, the correct answer is $\\text{IV}.$" 13194139533654 true] [17592186129630 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533654 true] [17592186129630 1145 "First, we notice that the function has precisely three distinct roots, $x=-3,$ $x=-1,$ and $x=2.$\n\nBased on the graph, we can make the following conclusions about the multiplicities of the roots:\n\n- Since the graph is *not* tangent to the $x$-axis at $x=-3,$ this root is a simple root (the multiplicity equals $1$).\n\n- Since the graph just touches the $x$-axis and reverses direction at $x=-1,$ this root has even multiplicity.\n\n- Since the graph is *not* tangent to the $x$-axis at $x=2,$ this root is a simple root (the multiplicity equals $1$).\n\nFrom the given options, the only possible equation is $y=(x+3)(x+1{)}^{2}(x-2).$" 13194139533654 true] [17592186244302 1017 #edb/ref 17592186244303 13194139533654 true] [17592186244302 1089 "q-112585" 13194139533654 true] [17592186244302 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533654 true] [17592186244302 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13932088/assets/5e4e2241490626139e15800f3b90c8e15a9963bdcace9907f8557b0897d533e6.png)\n\nThe graph of a polynomial $p(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $p(0)$ is positive.\n- $p(x)\\to -\\infty $ as $x\\to \\infty .$\n- $x=0$ is a root of odd multiplicity." 13194139533654 true] [17592186244302 1113 #uuid "bb9bcd3c-d3ff-52c3-b152-efe91ebd156b" 13194139533654 true] [17592186244302 1138 false 13194139533654 true] [17592186244302 1145 "Let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. The graph passes through the $y$-axis at $y=0,$ which means that $p(0)=0.$\n\n- Statement II is true. As $x$ moves right in the positive direction towards $\\infty ,$ the $y$ value moves down in the negative direction towards $-\\infty .$\n\n- Statement III is true. The root $x=0$ has an odd multiplicity because the graph passes through the $x$-axis at this point.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only\"." 13194139533654 true] [17592186244303 1000 #edb/ref 17592186244304 13194139533654 true] [17592186244303 1000 #edb/ref 17592186244305 13194139533654 true] [17592186244303 1000 #edb/ref 17592186244306 13194139533654 true] [17592186244303 1000 #edb/ref 17592186244307 13194139533654 true] [17592186244303 1000 #edb/ref 17592186244308 13194139533654 true] [17592186244303 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533654 true] [17592186244303 1041 "selection" 13194139533654 true] [17592186244303 1104 #edb/ref 17592186244304 13194139533654 true] [17592186244303 1110 #uuid "ca74bbcd-d88b-5d73-8d1a-a188a1e6bf5f" 13194139533654 true] [17592186244304 1034 "II and III only" 13194139533654 true] [17592186244304 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533654 true] [17592186244304 1111 #uuid "30216577-76e8-558c-b2e8-d8a6e5785df3" 13194139533654 true] [17592186244305 1034 "I and III only" 13194139533654 true] [17592186244305 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533654 true] [17592186244305 1111 #uuid "0bdc5fea-b91e-521e-9837-4b86b9defed2" 13194139533654 true] [17592186244306 1034 "I only" 13194139533654 true] [17592186244306 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533654 true] [17592186244306 1111 #uuid "909edf30-af3a-5009-ab35-d81d7ad0fba5" 13194139533654 true] [17592186244307 1034 "I and II only" 13194139533654 true] [17592186244307 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533654 true] [17592186244307 1111 #uuid "7670b3a6-e4e6-5019-a636-bae969fe728a" 13194139533654 true] [17592186244308 1034 "II only" 13194139533654 true] [17592186244308 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533654 true] [17592186244308 1111 #uuid "444816b0-ca8d-5e6a-8838-18877dbbfa59" 13194139533654 true] [17592186244309 1017 #edb/ref 17592186244310 13194139533654 true] [17592186244309 1089 "q-112595" 13194139533654 true] [17592186244309 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533654 true] [17592186244309 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13932088/assets/a5f078132f264a73a4cf2cd1c6f43c61e7f396fa0d6fc8daf2f979c3665ed83e.png)\n\nWhich of the following could be the equation of the polynomial curve shown above?" 13194139533654 true] [17592186244309 1113 #uuid "e12ebcfe-8743-5040-9b0a-16591b625086" 13194139533654 true] [17592186244309 1138 false 13194139533654 true] [17592186244309 1145 "First, we notice that the function has precisely three distinct roots, $x=-3,$ $x=0$ and $x=2.$\n\nBased on the graph, we can make the following conclusions about the multiplicities of the roots:\n\n- Since the graph is *not* tangent to the $x$-axis at $x=-3,$ this root is a simple root (the multiplicity equals $1$).\n\n- Since the graph just touches the $x$-axis and reverses direction at $x=0,$ this root has even multiplicity.\n\n- Since the graph just touches the $x$-axis and reverses direction at $x=2,$ this root has even multiplicity.\n\nTherefore, the only possible equation is $y=-\\frac{1}{10}{x}^{2}(x+3)(x-2{)}^{4}.$" 13194139533654 true] [17592186244310 1000 #edb/ref 17592186244311 13194139533654 true] [17592186244310 1000 