d3ae20db2c0742e8b.png)\n\nWhich of the following could be the equation of the polynomial curve shown above?" 13194139533654 true] [17592186244316 1113 #uuid "9b9ef6a9-d053-5b03-b082-12e556b7bb89" 13194139533654 true] [17592186244316 1138 false 13194139533654 true] [17592186244316 1145 "First, we notice that the function has precisely three distinct roots, $x=-2,$ $x=0$ and $x=3.$\n\nBased on the graph, we can make the following conclusions about the multiplicities of the roots:\n\n- Since the graph is *not* tangent to the $x$-axis at $x=-2,$ this root is a simple root (the multiplicity equals $1$).\n\n- Since the graph passes through $x=0,$ this root has odd multiplicity, and since the graph is tangent to the $x$-axis at $x=0,$ the multiplicity must be greater than one.\n\n- Since the graph is *not* tangent to the $x$-axis at $x=3,$ this root is a simple root (the multiplicity equals $1$).\n\nTherefore, the only possible equation is $y=\\frac{1}{5}{x}^{3}(x+2)(x-3).$" 13194139533654 true] [17592186244317 1000 #edb/ref 17592186244318 13194139533654 true] [17592186244317 1000 #edb/ref 17592186244319 13194139533654 true] [17592186244317 1000 #edb/ref 17592186244320 13194139533654 true] [17592186244317 1000 #edb/ref 17592186244321 13194139533654 true] [17592186244317 1000 #edb/ref 17592186244322 13194139533654 true] [17592186244317 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533654 true] [17592186244317 1041 "selection" 13194139533654 true] [17592186244317 1104 #edb/ref 17592186244318 13194139533654 true] [17592186244317 1110 #uuid "5a5966a9-00ad-5d27-a4a5-c8dada4b910f" 13194139533654 true] [17592186244318 1034 "y=\\frac{1}{5}{x}^{3}(x+2)(x-3)" 13194139533654 true] [17592186244318 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533654 true] [17592186244318 1111 #uuid "ecdc1492-a9ae-562a-b521-c0b305248e3e" 13194139533654 true] [17592186244319 1034 "y=5{x}^{3}(x+2{)}^{2}(x-3{)}^{3}" 13194139533654 true] [17592186244319 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533654 true] [17592186244319 1111 #uuid "ad46d746-7648-53a7-910c-7a7a8003261d" 13194139533654 true] [17592186244320 1034 "y=2x(x+2)(x+3{)}^{3}" 13194139533654 true] [17592186244320 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533654 true] [17592186244320 1111 #uuid "d16c0065-070a-5431-93eb-4c32cb6341cf" 13194139533654 true] [17592186244321 1034 "y=\\frac{1}{4}x(x+2)(x-3)" 13194139533654 true] [17592186244321 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533654 true] [17592186244321 1111 #uuid "288a0288-d8ec-5858-a4f1-555cef729f67" 13194139533654 true] [17592186244322 1034 "y={x}^{3}(x+2{)}^{3}(x+3{)}^{2}" 13194139533654 true] [17592186244322 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533654 true] [17592186244322 1111 #uuid "366403ac-7e1e-57d2-8139-b6563fe3ed21" 13194139533654 true] [17592186244323 1017 #edb/ref 17592186244324 13194139533654 true] [17592186244323 1089 "q-141133" 13194139533654 true] [17592186244323 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533654 true] [17592186244323 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13932088/assets/33bec46293f976bfb829e987cd3ce26fa5cc67310bc830fc8f46b7f0d57b3acf.png)\n\nA part of the graph of a polynomial $p(x)$ is given above. Which of the following statements is true?" 13194139533654 true] [17592186244323 1113 #uuid "8fd7fdcf-ee89-52e6-b0ff-f563716e91a3" 13194139533654 true] [17592186244323 1138 false 13194139533654 true] [17592186244323 1145 "| Multiplicity | Sketch | Sketch |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ \n\n (Simple Root) | ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13932088/assets/5a0221f93c65a1eeb4503604a558a6da81c4c7af81c2c52cc35ecf3d189fe99f.png) | ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13932088/assets/d556b4edf39181eebb7ec87c344d6f657a6875ecd2745405eaf5351ccaff6f96.png) |\n| $>1$ \n\n (Multiple Root) \n\n Even | ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13932088/assets/9d14c2588da2ee9c9f5bbc32a70791146bed609ea0d6d5912aeaa2edab02e2d7.png) | ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13932088/assets/28ad9248a9a509e3a44937d0438fb2366f550a22360d01b9b6a29bcac41357e8.png) |\n| $>1$ \n\n (Multiple Root) \n\n Odd | ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13932088/assets/3ae81378cb3c918dd5b7730519eb5a91e949f88eef9f03f4910f4ab8fb3cd5a9.png) | ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13932088/assets/9506a7ce4031f1f6244c0110c3235f66ea5652bb53548a032732869cf21f5997.png) |\n\nIt's possible to determine information regarding the multiplicities of the roots of a polynomial from its graph.\n\n- If the graph intersects the $x$-axis but is *not* tangent to the $x$-axis at $x=a,$ we have a *simple* root $x=a.$\n\n- If the graph is *tangent* to the $x$-axis at $x=a,$ we have a *multiple* root $x=a.$ Furthermore:\n\n- If the graph touches the $x$-axis and reverses direction at $x=a,$ the multiplicity of a root $x=a$ is *even*.\n\n- If the graph crosses the $x$-axis at $x=a,$ the multiplicity of a root $x=a$ is *odd*.\n\nIn our case, the graph is *tangent* to the $x$-axis at $x=-1.$ So, $x=-1$ is a *multiple* root. \n\nFurthermore, the graph crosses the $x$-axis at $x=-1.$ So, $x=-1$ has *odd* multiplicity.\n\nTherefore, $x=-1$ is a multiple root of odd multiplicity." 13194139533654 true] [17592186244324 1000 #edb/ref 17592186244325 13194139533654 true] [17592186244324 1000 #edb/ref 17592186244326 13194139533654 true] [17592186244324 1000 #edb/ref 17592186244327 13194139533654 true] [17592186244324 1000 #edb/ref 17592186244328 13194139533654 true] [17592186244324 1000 #edb/ref 17592186244329 13194139533654 true] [17592186244324 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533654 true] [17592186244324 1041 "selection" 13194139533654 true] [17592186244324 1104 #edb/ref 17592186244326 13194139533654 true] [17592186244324 1110 #uuid "b74d81c4-5ae4-5ce8-b92e-f7728a632b30" 13194139533654 true] [17592186244325